:"ARI","distance":0.7295,"shared":12,"play_caller":"Drew Petzing"},{"season":2023,"team":"CAR","distance":0.8056,"shared":12,"play_caller":"Frank Reich"},{"season":2022,"team":"NYG","distance":0.808,"shared":12,"play_caller":"Mike Kafka"}],"defense":[{"season":2023,"team":"SF","distance":0.07801,"shared":4,"play_caller":null},{"season":2024,"team":"PHI","distance":0.1904,"shared":4,"play_caller":"Vic Fangio"},{"season":2023,"team":"BUF","distance":0.2653,"shared":4,"play_caller":null},{"season":2023,"team":"MIA","distance":0.3599,"shared":4,"play_caller":"Vic Fangio"},{"season":2025,"team":"BUF","distance":0.3649,"shared":4,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Titans are 1-5 through week 6 of the 2026 season and have been outscored 129 to 84. Neither side has held up so far, with the offense 31st in EPA per play and the defense 23rd in EPA allowed. What sets them apart is the 3rd highest no-huddle rate in the league.","offense":"The Titans offense has struggled, ranking 25th or worse in three of the five main efficiency measures. They have been one of the most run-heavy teams in the league, passing less often than game situations would predict. Schematically, they stand out for the 3rd highest no-huddle rate and a pre-snap motion rate that was among the lowest in the league. Cam Ward has run the passing game and leaned heavily on Carnell Tate, who has drawn 27% of the targets.","defense":"The Titans defense has been well below average by EPA allowed per play so far. It has been much better against the run than the pass, ranking 15th and 27th. Jeffery Simmons has been as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise is Jacob Martin, who has 3.5 sacks while playing only 57% of the snaps, after not being a regular on this defense last season. Their scheme has been fairly conventional, and the closest thing to a signature is an eight man box rate that ranks 8th lowest in the league.","special":"The Titans have been one of the most conservative teams on fourth down, going for it on only 0% of their short-yardage chances. Their kickers have made 90% of field goals, which is a little above average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the defensive line, about 25% of the money on the books. Their linebackers were among the youngest in the league.","players":"Cam Ward has played nearly every snap at quarterback and thrown for 998 yards. Carnell Tate has been the clear top target with 27% of the looks and 344 receiving yards. Tony Pollard has handled most of the work in the backfield with 76% of the carries. On defense, Jeffery Simmons leads the team with 4 sacks and Amani Hooker has picked off 2 passes.","coaching":"Brian Daboll is calling the offense and Robert Saleh is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2025 and 2023 Giants. Their defense looks most like the 2023 49ers and the 2024 Eagles under Vic Fangio.","trends":"The offense went from 30th in EPA per play in 2025 to 31st so far this season, about where it was. The defense moved from 28th in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 23rd so far this season, a modest improvement. The biggest change in approach was screen rate, which went from 11% to 6%.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}